**Proposition 159.2 (Joint endpoint feasibility and completion).** Every
row of Definition 159.1 is a nonnegative allocation satisfying (154.1),
all 190 cap rows at its vertex, and

$$
p_{1^6}(0)=1-e_v.
\tag{159.1}
$$

The total number of nonzero terminal numerators is 4936 and the largest
endpoint denominator is 258. Completing each pair by

$$
(T^{N,00},T^{N,01},T^{N,10},T^{N,11})
=(T^{O,0},T^{O,0},T^{O,10},T^{O,11})
\tag{159.2}
$$

and leaving empty and singleton cells unchanged gives a full $N$
allocation with the identical word laws. Each endpoint kernel belongs
to $\mathscr C_6$ of Definition 108.2.

**Proof.** All listed integers are positive, and all unlisted entries
are zero. The formulas below give finite integer identities and
inequalities for the whole table, without choosing separate mixtures.
Write $u_{v,c,r}$ for the listed integers and let
$q_{v,b,r}=\sum_Su_{v,c_O(b,S),r}$. If $c_S$ is a schedule's first cell,
substitution in the table gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_ru_{v,c,r}-\sum_ru_{v,c_S,r}&=0\quad(c\text{ a cell of }S),\\
\sum_S\sum_ru_{v,c_S,r}&=D_v,\\
\sum_{r\in E_j}q_{v,b_j,r}
-D_vk_j-\sum_{i=1}^6t_{ji}(D_va_i)+D_ve_v&\ge0,\\
q_{v,1^6,0}&=D_v-D_ve_v.
\end{aligned}
\tag{159.3}
$$

The first line has 36 nontrivial cell differences per endpoint; the
second normalizes its 22 schedule masses. The third consists of 190
integer inequalities per endpoint, with minimum slack zero in the
complete collection. Thus (159.3) gives 5616 cell differences, 156
budget identities and 29640 TV inequalities, all determined by the
primitive tables and their explicit row-selection formulas.

In (159.2) the completed $N$ cell selected by any word is exactly the
selected $O$ cell. Hence all seven coordinates agree for every word;
there are 9984 endpoint/word vector equalities. Equal masses remain
equal after duplication, and the global normalization is unchanged.
This proves law equality by construction, without resampling.

The stopped formula in (140.8) shows degree at most two. The already
proved [Theorem 109.1](KBONACCI_SELF_CALIBRATING_ACQUISITION_RESOURCES_CONTINUATION.md#109-an-exact-coefficient-criterion-with-one-common-probability-budget)
therefore gives $c\ge H$ and $\beta\le1$. These conditions can also be
